In simple terms, we are okay to proceed in SA and Nigeria, but Kenya will require further compliance activities (submission of the DPIA to their authority), unless we decide to reclassify the DPIA as not being high risk. Hope this is helpful!

*Kenya*

{color:#bf2600}Content of DPIA: {color}{{Generally compliant}}{color:#bf2600}.{color}

The [Guidance Note|https://www.odpc.go.ke/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/ODPC-guidance-note-on-Data-Protection-Impact-assessment.pdf] issued by the Kenyan Data Protection Commissioner requires the following be reflected in the DPIA:

* the amount of personal data collected;
* the extent of processing of the personal data;
* the period and method of storage of the personal data and its accessibiIity;
* *the state of technological development available for processing* ([_@silja.hundt_|https://taxify.slack.com/team/U014BAEJSBT] _- we have not explicitly set this out in the DPIA, particularly when it comes to potential for interception of audio in transit - what are your thoughts?_);
* the special risks that exist in the processing of the data;
* a systematic description of the envisaged processing operations and the purposes of the processing, including, where applicable, the legitimate interest pursued by the data controller or data processor;
* an assessment of the necessity and proportionality of the processing operations in relation to the purposes;
* an assessment of the risks to the rights and freedoms of data subjects; and the measures envisaged to address the risks and the safeguards, security measures and mechanisms to ensure the protection of personal data and to demonstrate compliance with this Act, taking into account the rights, and legitimate interests of data subjects and other persons concerned (section 31(1)(d).

{color:#bf2600}Submission of DPIA to Kenya's Data Protection Commissioner?: {color}{{Required.}}

*If we are concluding that the DPIA is high risk* (_it is highlighted in yellow in the DPIA and not sure if this is final?_), *then we are required to submit to the Regulator in Kenya 60 days prior to beginning the processing for prior authorisation* (this is explicit in the Act) - any launch of the feature in Kenya needs to take this into account from a timing perspective. However, [~accountid:5ecceffac761b10c33d5ba08] I do note that the type of processing activities undertaken as part of this feature (secret audio recording) do not fall within the (non-exhaustive) types of processing activities which are automatically deemed high-risk by the Regulations and Kenya’s Data Protection Commissioner. 

{quote}Section 31(3) of the [Act|https://www.odpc.go.ke/download/kenya-gazette-data-protection-act-2019/?wpdmdl=3235&refresh=6481afbfc08201686220735] states that "_The data controller or data processor shall consult the Data Commissioner prior to the processing if a data protection impact assessment prepared under this section indicates that the processing of the data would result in a high risk to the rights and freedoms of a data subject._" The Regulator's [Guidance Note|https://www.odpc.go.ke/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/ODPC-guidance-note-on-Data-Protection-Impact-assessment.pdf] on DPIAs states that "_a DPIA must be submitted 60 days prior to the commencement of the processing of personal data"_{quote}

*South Africa*

{color:#bf2600}Content of DPIA{color}: {{No criteria set out in South Africa's data protection law. Content is sufficient}}.

{color:#bf2600}Submission of DPIA to the Information Regulator{color}: {{Not necessary}}

{color:#bf2600}RICA{color}: South Africa's RICA law would also entitle recording of audio by one party to a conversation. The Data Protection Law would not prohibit the recording either: {{fine to proceed under RICA}}

*Nigeria*

{color:#bf2600}Content of DPIA: {color}{{Compliant}}{color:#bf2600}.{color}

[Nigeria Data Protection Regulation Implementation Framework|https://nitda.gov.ng/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/NDPR-Implementation-Framework.pdf] sets out the following criteria:

* a description of the envisaged processing operations;
* the purposes of the processing;
* the legitimate interest pursued by the controller;
* an assessment of the necessity and proportionality of the processing operations in relation to the purposes;
* an assessment of the risks to the rights and freedoms of data subject; and
* risk mitigation measures being proposed to address the risk.

{color:#bf2600}Submission of DPIA to the Nigeria Data Protection Bureau:  {color}{{Not necessary, but they can request it}}

{color:#bf2600}Nigeria's Lawful Interception of Communications Regulations, 2019{color}{{: fine to proceed under the Regulations}}{color:#bf2600}: {color}the Regulations permit any party to a communication can intercept the communication (which includes to record it) where:

{quote}_(b) it is done by a person who is a party to the communication, and has sufficient reason to believe that there is a threat to human life and safety ; and_
_(c) in the ordinary course of business, it is required to record or monitor such communication_{quote}
